FujiNet Go Apple II (desktop) -- rolling notes
==============================================

Follows the 10-step order of work in fujinet-go-adam-desktop/PORTING.md §12.

Done (this pass)
----------------

M1 -- core + GNOME frontend, emulator half only:

* Repo skeleton, GPL-3.0, two pinned submodules:
    third_party/applewin           FujiNetWIFI/AppleWin @ 25e8ddd (branch linux)
    third_party/fujinet-firmware   FujiNetWIFI/fujinet-firmware @ 7a994ab
* cmake/Dependencies.cmake -- apple2_provide_dependency(), 3-step resolution
  (<NAME>_SRC override -> submodule -> clone the pin), sentinel files, pin
  drift warning.
* cmake/StageAppleWin.cmake -- copies source/resource/libyaml/minizip/bin
  into core/applewin-generated (file(COPY), not rsync: Windows), then runs
  tools/applewin/patch-staged-tree.py through ${Python3_EXECUTABLE}. MSYS2
  CMake cannot execute_process a .sh, but a native python is fine.
  .source-info records the source commit + a hash of the patch script, so
  editing a transform re-stages. -DAPPLEWIN_RESTAGE=ON forces it.
* tools/applewin/patch-staged-tree.py -- the 13 transforms, ported from the
  Android build-applewin-core.sh. All idempotent, all hard-fail on a missing
  anchor. NOTE: the idempotency test must check the REPLACEMENT before the
  anchor -- several transforms wrap their anchor rather than replacing it, so
  anchor-first re-applies them (nested Boost if(), duplicated slot7 block).
* core/ -- libapple2session:
    applewin/apple2_host.{cpp,h}  the libretro frontend, ported from Android
                                  (android/log.h replaced by a log-sink seam)
    src/session.c                 paced emulator thread, vsync phase-lock
    src/input_map.c               X11 keysym -> libretro (keycode, char, mods)
    src/{settings,paths,audio_sdl,gamepad_sdl,fujinet_runtime}.c
  Differences from the ADAM target, all deliberate:
    - frames are XRGB8888 uint32, not RGB565
    - geometry is NOT fixed: copy_frame reports w/h, because a VidHD card in
      slot 3 switches the core between 560x384 and 640x400
    - audio is interleaved stereo, not mono (FujiNet's SAM overlay is mono and
      is mixed into both channels)
    - the joystick is a pair of analog paddles, not a digital stick
* frontends/gnome -- GTK4 + libadwaita, XRGB8888 straight into a
  GdkMemoryTexture (no per-pixel conversion), aspect TV 4:3 / square / integer,
  preferences over the shared settings store.
  NOTE: Alt is NOT passed to the window manager here (unlike the ADAM
  frontend) -- the Alt keys are Open Apple and Closed Apple.
* ctest: input (key + paddle mapping), boot_smoke (headless core boot),
  desktop_entry_gnome, metainfo_gnome. All passing.
* COMPLIANCE.md.

Verified: Enhanced Apple //e boots to its banner in the GNOME window at
60 fps with 60/60 frames on vsync (APPLE2_PACE_LOG=1).

Findings worth remembering
--------------------------

* The AppleWin libretro core's DEFAULT video_mode is "Color (RGB Card/Monitor)",
  which emulates an RGB card. With no such card fitted it renders a blank text
  screen as blue horizontal banding instead of black -- the boot screen is
  unreadable, and it looks exactly like a framebuffer/stride bug. Every other
  mode renders correctly. We default to "Color (Composite Monitor)" and expose
  the setting. Worth reporting upstream.
* createRetroRegistry() returns an EMPTY registry, so anything not covered by
  a core option falls back to AppleWin's internal default. There is no slot6
  core option, so the Disk ][ in slot 6 is not configurable from here.
* DebugGetVideoMode() returns the *debugger's* video-mode override, not the
  machine's -- it leaves the output untouched when the debugger is inactive.
  Use Video::GetVideoMode() via GetVideo() to read the real one.

M2 -- clone-and-build verified two ways:
  * a plain `git clone` (no --recurse-submodules) auto-fetches the submodules
    and builds; ctest green.
  * a tree with .git and third_party/* removed takes the clone-the-pin branch
    of apple2_provide_dependency and builds; ctest green.

M3 -- FujiNet in-process. DONE: CONFIG BOOTS.

  Working:
  * tools/fujinet/build-fujinet-desktop.sh forked from adam's, PC_TARGET=APPLE,
    [BOIP] port=1985, web UI 8000. All 11 target-independent patch blocks kept;
    the two ADAM portability patches replaced by the two APPLE ones
    (fnFsSPIFFS.cpp + mgHttpClient.cpp root their paths absolutely from
    FUJINET_RUNTIME_ROOT, because AppleWin's SetCurrentImageDir() chdir()s and
    moves the shared process CWD out from under FujiNet). Builds first try.
  * libfujinet.so exports exactly the six-symbol contract, 8 symbols total --
    the ELF version script is doing its job.
  * The runtime loads in-process, mounts SPIFFS and SD from absolute paths,
    reads our fnconfig.ini, mounts /autorun.po, and attaches to the bus.
  * SmartPort/SLIP is live: LISTEN 127.0.0.1:1985 plus an ESTABLISHED pair
    whose BOTH endpoints belong to our own PID, with iwm_slip::encode_packet
    traffic and "handling init command" / "Sending INIT Response Packet".

  Two real defects found and fixed along the way:
  * The AppleWin libretro frontend NEVER STARTS THE SLIP LISTENER. Upstream
    starts it in LoadConfiguration() and in the Windows property sheet; the
    libretro path reaches neither, and the card ctor only logs. FujiNet dials
    forever and nothing answers. Staging patch 13 starts it from the
    SmartPortOverSlip constructor -- inserting the card is when its listener
    should come up, and patch 8/9 already makes start() safe over a previous
    listener. Patch 14 changes default_listener_address to 127.0.0.1: upstream
    binds 0.0.0.0, which exposes an unauthenticated block-device channel to
    the network, and the default is what LoadConfiguration re-initialises from.
    BOTH BELONG UPSTREAM.
  * Startup order is the OPPOSITE of ADAM's, and it is not cosmetic. FujiNet's
    APPLE build blocks inside setup (iwm_slip::setup_spi) until the SLIP
    connection is made. Starting it first (as adam does) deadlocks the whole
    session: start_runtime never returns, the emulator thread is never created,
    the listener never binds, and FujiNet waits forever. The session now starts
    the emulator, waits on a condvar for the core to report itself up, and only
    then starts FujiNet. The Android app does the same. PORTING.md §2 lists
    Apple2 as having "forgiving" ordering like ADAM -- that is wrong, and the
    guide is worth correcting.

  * A THIRD defect, and the one that actually kept CONFIG from booting: our
    own adopt_opt() copied with snprintf(dst, n, "%s", value) where dst and
    value could be THE SAME BUFFER -- default_opts() hands back
    opts->slotN pointing straight at s->opt_slotN, so start() aliased them.
    Overlapping snprintf is undefined; glibc left the option empty, our
    GET_VARIABLE returns false for an empty value, and the core silently fell
    back to its own defaults -- slot 7 "Empty", so no FujiNet card at all.
    Being UB it was INTERMITTENT, which is why the bus sometimes came up.
    adopt_opt now copies from the option table's static string, never from
    the caller's pointer.

  Verified end to end: the Apple //e boots FujiNet CONFIG -- host list,
  SmartPort drive table and the full menu bar -- with the SLIP bus carrying
  the traffic. ctest green.

  Follow-ups:
  * fnconfig.ini is still the Android app's ATARI default (hsioindex,
    [Cassette], enable_apetime, and Atari TNFS hosts, which is what CONFIG's
    host list shows). Curate it for Apple II.
  * Web UI port 8000 collides with a standalone fujinet-pc-atari on this
    machine; FUJINET_WEBUI_BIND overrides it.
  * A new "slot6" core option was added (upstream has none; AppleWin
    hard-inserts a Disk ][ in CardManager's constructor) so the Disk ][ can be
    removed. Default "Disk II".

M4 -- partly done:
  * fnconfig.ini now comes from the firmware's own BUILD_APPLE default, so
    CONFIG lists SD / tnfs.fujinet.online / apps.irata.online instead of the
    Atari host list.
  * WITH_APPLE_ROMS=OFF is implemented. tools/applewin/generate-rom-loader.py
    generates a replacement apple2roms target that reads firmware images from
    the user's ROM directory at first use; the id->filename table is parsed
    out of AppleWin's resource/CMakeLists.txt so it cannot drift. The split is
    by kind (.rom/.ROM/.bin go out, artwork and fonts stay), with
    spoverslip.bin kept embedded so FujiNet works with no ROMs at all.
    "Import System ROMs..." copies files in and restarts. A no_embedded_roms
    ctest greps the binary for a slice of every firmware image, so the
    COMPLIANCE.md claim is enforced rather than asserted.
    Verified: ROM-less + imported ROMs boots CONFIG identically to the
    embedded build.
  * slot6 is now configurable (see above).

M5 -- KDE frontend done. Plain Qt6 Widgets, no KDE Frameworks. QOpenGLWidget
  with swapInterval 1, whose frameSwapped signal both feeds the session's
  vsync phase-lock and schedules the next repaint. Boots CONFIG; ctest covers
  its desktop entry and metainfo.
  The point of doing this early was to prove the core is toolkit-agnostic,
  and it did: the KDE frontend needed NO change to core/ at all.
  Two Qt-specific things worth remembering:
  * quint64 and uint64_t are distinct types here (unsigned long long vs
    unsigned long), so the frame serial must be declared uint64_t.
  * QImage's stride is not the session's. The frame is kept in a plain
    std::vector<uint32_t> and QImage is only ever a VIEW over it with an
    explicit bytesPerLine, because the session packs rows at the frame width.
  * ShortcutOverride is accepted for everything except F10/F11 -- Alt must not
    reach the menu bar, it is Open/Closed Apple.

Milestone notes (M4 onward)
---------------------------
M4  remaining: gamepad/paddle testing against real hardware, audio check.
M6  debugger. THE SEAM IS DONE AND TESTED; the engine and windows remain.
    The earlier worry -- that AppleWin executes per-cycle with no
    single-instruction entry point at the libretro boundary -- was WRONG.
    CPU.cpp documents it in so many words:
        // uCycles:
        //  =0  : Do single step
        //  >0  : Do multi-opcode emulation
    So the seam is just:
      step      CpuExecute(0, true)          -- plain free function
      registers `regs` (regsrec, CPU.h)      -- plain global
      memory    `mem` (Memory.h)             -- the CPU's ACTIVE 64K view
    apple2host_{get,set}_regs / _step_instruction / _{read,write}_mem expose
    these, and core/tests/debug_seam_test.c proves them: PC advances one
    instruction at a time within a sane cycle count, registers round-trip
    without disturbing PC, ROM at $FF00 reads as ROM, RAM round-trips, and
    reading $C000 does not toggle a soft switch or jam the CPU.
    NOTE: read through `mem`, NOT MemGetMainPtr() -- the latter returns the
    main RAM image, so $FF00 would read as RAM instead of the ROM the 6502 is
    actually executing.
    ENGINE AND DISASSEMBLER NOW DONE TOO:
      core/debugger/m6502dasm.c  fresh table-driven 6502/65C02 decoder, the
        full 65C02 matrix. Opcodes the 65C02 does not define decode as 1-byte
        "???" rather than as NMOS undocumented instructions -- on a 65C02
        those slots really are NOPs, and a plausible NMOS mnemonic would be
        actively misleading. dasm_test sweeps all 256.
      core/debugger/debugger.c   pause/resume, step into/over/out, run-to,
        breakpoints, disassembly, symbols. Everything touching core state runs
        on the EMULATOR thread; UI calls only set flags and wait.
        "Engaged" swaps the session's whole-frame call for a stepped loop, so
        a session that never opens the debugger pays one volatile read a frame.
      debug_engine_test drives it from a UI thread against a real running
        machine and checks the disassembled lengths tile the address range.
    Symbols load from AppleWin's own APPLE2E.SYM / A2_BASIC.SYM / A2_DOS33.SYM2,
    which staging already copies in -- no extraction script for this target.
    The GNOME debugger window is done: run control, 6502 registers with a
    decoded flag string, live disassembly with click-to-toggle breakpoints and
    a PC marker, and a memory view that takes an address OR a symbol name.
    F12 opens it, F5/F7/F8/Shift+F8 drive it, APPLE2_OPEN_DEBUGGER=1 opens it
    at launch. It opens PAUSED on purpose -- a debugger that opens on a
    running machine shows a listing that is stale before it is drawn.
    NOTE: the engine's stop callback fires on the EMULATOR thread, so it is
    marshalled with g_idle_add before any widget is touched.
    NOTE: AppleWin stores SP as a full stack address ($01F9), not the 6502's
    8-bit value.
    The KDE debugger window is done too, same shape over the same engine,
    with the stop callback handed to the GUI thread by a queued connection
    (QMetaObject::invokeMethod) instead of g_idle_add.
    NOTE for any future Qt pane: under a tiling window manager the window is
    whatever width the tile is, resize() is ignored, and a QScrollArea still
    propagates its child's minimum -- so the disassembly needs an explicit
    setMinimumWidth or the register/memory panes crowd it out entirely.
    Remaining for M6: Apple II video views (text / lo-res / hi-res page
    decoders) to replace adam's vdpview.c.
M7  CI: linux, then macOS and Windows, then flatpak.
    Top Windows risk: the AppleWin libretro frontend has never been built
    under MinGW (the cross-compile work upstream covers the SDL2 frontend).
M8 -- icons done. Rendered by tools/icons/make-icons.py from the Android
  app's own adaptive-icon foreground
  (fujinet-go-apple2/app/src/main/res/mipmap-xxxhdpi/ic_launcher_adaptive_fore.png,
  copied verbatim to data/icons/src/) over the brand red #F44336 from the same
  app's values/colors.xml, with Android's rounded mask baked in. Results are
  committed so building needs no image tooling.
  This turned up that `cmake --install` was BROKEN and nothing noticed: the
  install rules referenced an icon set that did not exist. CI now runs
  cmake --install and checks for the desktop entries, metainfo, icons, symbol
  tables and the binary.
  M8 is done: screenshots are captured (data/screenshots/) and referenced from
  both metainfo files -- CONFIG running on GNOME, the 6502 debugger, and
  CONFIG on KDE. appstreamcli --pedantic passes.
M9 -- packaging DONE.
    Flatpak manifests for both frontends; every input declared (SDL3, Boost
    headers, mbedTLS 3.6.5 + the threading sed, the PyYAML/Jinja2/MarkupSafe
    sdists AND flit_core -- Jinja2's build backend, which the GNOME SDK
    happens to ship and the KDE one does not).
    Dropping the xxd dependency (tools/applewin/xxd-i.py, byte-identical to
    real xxd) removed it from the flatpak SDKs, from apt, and from MSYS2 all
    at once, and got rid of a shell redirection native Windows cmake choked
    on.
    NSIS installer: per-user install into %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs, no UAC.
    Its welcome page says outright that the ROMs are not included -- the
    difference between "one more step" and "I got a black screen".
    Release job: tag-gated, refuses to publish unless the tag, the VERSION in
    CMakeLists.txt and every metainfo <release> agree; creates a DRAFT with
    --generate-notes.
    COMPLIANCE: every artifact CI uploads is built WITH_APPLE_ROMS=OFF, and
    the OFF is pinned in the file that DEFINES the artifact (the two flatpak
    manifests, the windows CI job), not in the command that invokes it. A
    CMake default is a convenience; it is not a distribution policy, and
    "remember to pass -DWITH_APPLE_ROMS=OFF when cutting a release" is the
    kind of rule that gets forgotten exactly once. The linux job still builds
    ON so the embedded path cannot rot.

M10 -- Windows frontend DONE; macOS remains.
    frontends/windows: Win32/GDI over the shared session. Differences from
    the ADAM frontend it is modelled on, both deliberate:
      - no pixel conversion. The session hands out XRGB8888, which is
        byte-for-byte what a 32bpp BI_RGB DIB wants, so StretchDIBits reads
        the session's buffer directly (ADAM expands RGB565 by hand).
      - geometry is not fixed: the frame's own dimensions drive the letterbox,
        because VidHD in slot 3 switches the core 560x384 <-> 640x400.
    Keyboard is driven entirely from WM_KEYDOWN/WM_KEYUP, never WM_CHAR: the
    core tracks Open/Closed Apple as HELD modifiers, so every key needs a
    matching release and WM_CHAR has none. The printable character comes from
    ToUnicode() with Ctrl masked OUT of the key state -- otherwise it folds
    Ctrl in and returns a control code, and the mapping table wants the plain
    character (it zeroes it for Ctrl combos itself, after deriving the
    keycode). Flag bit 2 keeps ToUnicode from eating dead-key state.
    WM_SYSCHAR is swallowed so Alt does not ding or open the menu bar (Alt is
    Open/Closed Apple); Alt+F4 is let through explicitly. WM_KILLFOCUS lifts
    both Apple keys -- releases go to the focused window, so Alt+Tab would
    otherwise leave Open Apple stuck down.
    The machine/video/slot menus are GENERATED from the option names the core
    registers, so a card added upstream appears with no edit here.
    Win32 debugger window: same shape as the GNOME/KDE ones over the same
    engine, stop callback marshalled with PostMessage. A LISTBOX for the
    disassembly (a click resolves to a row directly), 16 memory columns
    rather than 8 -- the 8 elsewhere is a tiling-WM concession that does not
    apply to a window that sets its own size.
    Validated by cross-compiling (cmake/toolchains/mingw-w64.cmake) and
    running under wine: boots to the //e banner, and the debugger shows
    WAIT3/NXTA4/HEADR resolved from APPLE2E.SYM with the flag string and the
    text-page ASCII column both correct.
    NOTE: cross-compiling needs a Boost include dir containing NOTHING ELSE.
    Passing /usr/include puts host glibc on the path as -isystem and the
    build dies on conflicting ssize_t/time_t against mingw's corecrt; a
    directory holding just a `boost` symlink is the fix.
    This also turned up a REAL bug in core/CMakeLists.txt: AppleWin's minizip
    includes "zlib.h" but its CMakeLists never adds ZLIB_INCLUDE_DIRS. Free
    wherever zlib is on the default include path (Linux /usr/include, MSYS2
    /ucrt64/include), fatal the moment it is not.
    NOTE: the exe imports WSOCK32.dll (legacy Winsock 1.1) as well as
    WS2_32 -- the CI import-table whitelist needs both.

Next
----
M6 -- DONE. core/debugger/a2video.c decodes text, lo-res and hi-res, pages 1
    and 2, to XRGB8888, with a pane in all four debugger windows.
    The whole file is one idea: Apple II screen rows are INTERLEAVED, so row 1
    is not 40 bytes after row 0 (Woz did that so the video counter fell out of
    a few gates instead of an address adder). A decoder that gets it wrong
    still draws a plausible picture, just scrambled -- so video_test pins the
    three text bands and hi-res's extra scanline interleave specifically, and
    the test was mutation-checked: naive linear rows, a dropped hi-res
    interleave and swapped lo-res nibbles are each caught.
    tools/debugger/genfont.py holds the 64 character-generator glyphs as ASCII
    art and prints a render-back so they can be eyeballed; the generated table
    is committed, as the icons are.
    KNOWN DIVERGENCE: the font is the ORIGINAL 64-glyph character generator,
    so lowercase folds to uppercase -- the view can read APPLE //E where the
    machine's own screen reads Apple //e. Rendering that faithfully means
    tracking the ALTCHARSET soft switch, and this is deliberately a page
    inspector rather than a second display emulator.
    LAYOUT NOTE: stacking registers + memory + video in one column does not
    survive a tiling window manager -- the bottom pane falls off, and an
    enclosing QScrollArea did NOT reliably rescue it. Qt tabs memory and video
    instead; Win32 and AppKit give the video pane a fixed slot at the bottom
    and let the memory dump take what is left; GTK scrolls the column.
    APPLE2_DEBUGGER_VIEW=N opens on a given page (0 text 1, 4 hi-res 1), which
    is also how the panes were verified: GNOME and KDE show the FujiNet splash
    decoded out of $2000, and the Win32 one under wine shows APPLE //E decoded
    out of $0400.
M10 macOS: WRITTEN, verified only by the macOS CI job -- there is no Mac here.
    frontends/macos: AppKit over the shared session. Same two deliberate
    differences as the Win32 one: no pixel conversion (XRGB8888 is exactly
    what a CGImage asked for kCGBitmapByteOrder32Little |
    kCGImageAlphaNoneSkipFirst reads, so the session's buffer goes straight
    to CoreGraphics), and geometry driven by the frame's own dimensions.
    CVDisplayLink for the vsync ticks -- deprecated in macOS 15 in favour of
    NSView.displayLink (14+), kept on purpose because plenty of Intel Macs
    never see macOS 14.
    THE APPLE KEYS ARE THE OPTION KEYS, NOT COMMAND. Command is how every
    menu shortcut on the system is typed and swallowing it would cost the
    user Cmd-Q and Cmd-W. Modifiers never produce keyDown:/keyUp: on macOS --
    they arrive as flagsChanged: with no press/release of their own, so the
    held state is tracked by hand from event.keyCode (0x3A left, 0x3D right).
    The two key codes are spelled out rather than pulled from Carbon: linking
    a whole framework for two integers is not a trade worth making.
    Settings popups are generated from the core's own option names, like the
    Win32 menus.
    NOTE for any future AppKit pane: ARC will NOT let you pass &ivar to an
    NSTextView ** out-parameter (the ivar is __strong, the parameter is
    __autoreleasing). Return the scroll view and pick the text view back up
    from .documentView instead.
    The bundle is UNSIGNED; Gatekeeper blocks it on first launch. Signing and
    notarisation want an Apple Developer identity in the repo secrets --
    fujinet-go-adam-desktop's macos job has the step to copy when one exists.
Upstream to FujiNetWIFI/AppleWin -- FOUR fixes, not three as earlier notes
    said (the audio one belongs on the list too):
      1. the SmartPort-over-SLIP listener is never started by the libretro
         frontend (LoadConfiguration() is the only caller and that frontend
         never calls it). THIS is what stood between "boots" and "FujiNet
         works".
      2. the listener defaults to 0.0.0.0 rather than loopback -- an
         unauthenticated block-device channel into the machine, on every
         interface. Must be the DEFAULT that changes: LoadConfiguration()
         re-initialises from it when the registry carries no address.
      3. std::memcpy without <cstring> -- fine via libstdc++ on Linux/macOS,
         fails on MinGW.
      4. rdirectsound.cpp mixes the second ring-buffer segment at
         buffer.data() instead of after the first (ptr passed by value,
         mixBuffer does *ptr += ...), so segments sum on every wrap.
    1 and 2 silently affect the Android port too -- it shares the frontend.
    Four clean commits are prepared; NOT submitted (that is the maintainer's
    call). Once they land, drop the matching transforms from
    tools/applewin/patch-staged-tree.py -- and note that building this repo
    against a branch carrying them would DOUBLE the listener start, because
    the patcher would not recognise its own replacement under different
    comment wording.
